r = \frac{\arcsin (\sin 2 \alpha)}{\arcsin (\sin \alpha)} = \frac{\arcsin (\sin 7 \alpha)}{\arcsin (\sin 2 \alpha)} = \frac{\arcsin (\sin t \alpha)}{\arcsin (\sin 7 \alpha)}
\] Since \(\arcsin (\sin x) = x\) for \(x \in [0, \frac{\pi}{2}]\), we can simplify the equation to: \[
r = \frac{2\alpha}{\alpha} = \frac{7\alpha}{2\alpha} = \frac{t\alpha}{7\alpha} \implies r = 2 \implies r = \frac{7}{2} \implies r = \frac{t}{7}
\] From the first two equalities, we get: \[
2 = \frac{7\alpha}{2\alpha} \implies 2 = \frac{7}{2} \text{ which is not true.}
\] Using the second and third equalities, we get: \[
\frac{7}{2} = \frac{t}{7} \implies t = \frac{49}{2}
\] So, the least positive value of \(t\) is \(\frac{49}{2}\). </think> <answer> \(\boxed{\frac{49}{2}}\) </answer>
